 :  Aluki Kotierk  Armando Muyolema  Diego A. Titueña Matango  Teresa L. 
 McCarty  &#160\;  Conference on Global and Indigenous Studies (CGIS) is m
 ade possible by the support from:&#160\;African Studies Program (https://
 africanstudies.indiana.edu/index.html?_gl=1*4pv3u5*_gcl_au*MTI2NDk4MTcxLj
 E3NzUwNTYwNjQ.*_ga*MTUxNzYzNjMwOS4xNzc1MDU2MDUw*_ga_61CH0D2DQW*czE3NzY5Nj
 g4MDEkbzMzJGcwJHQxNzc2OTY4ODAxJGo2MCRsMCRoMA..)Arts and Humanities Future
 s – Indigenous Futures at IUB (https://ahfutures.iu.edu/)Center for Lat
 in American and Caribbean Studies (https://clacs.indiana.edu/index.html)C
 enter for the Study of Global Change (https://global.indiana.edu/index.ht
 ml)Center for the Study of the Middle East (https://csme.indiana.edu/inde
 x.html)East Asian Studies Center (https://easc.indiana.edu/index.html)Inn
 er Asian and Uralic National Resource Center (https://iaunrc.indiana.edu/
 index.html)The Byrnes Institute (REEI) (https://reei.indiana.edu/index.ht
 ml)      &#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>CGIS 2026</strong> is a transdisc
 iplinary event that will bring together national and international schola
 rs\, educators\, practitioners\, students\, policy makers\, activists\, a
 cademic institutions\, Indigenous organizations\, grassroots organization
 s\, governmental and non-governmental organizations. The participants in 
 this conference will be involved in a local and global dialogue and excha
 nge of ideas\, research\, and experiences on the themes of the event.</p>
 <p>  Across the globe\, ethnic minorities and Indigenous communities have
  consistently strived to protect their rich cultural heritages and lingui
 stic nuances from the influences of colonial powers\, expanding nation-st
 ates\, and the homogenizing impact of globalization. This collective effo
 rt is increasingly recognized\, highlighted by the initiation of <a class
 ="external" href="https://www.unesco.org/en/decades/indigenous-languages/
 community/adhocgroups">UNESCO's "Indigenous Languages Decade" (2022-2032)
 </a>. The imperative acknowledgment is that Indigenous languages\, along 
 with the intricate knowledge systems interwoven within them\, stand as cr
 ucial pillars for preserving both biological and cultural pluralism.</p><
 p><em><strong><a href="https://app.oxfordabstracts.com/auth?redirect=/sta
 ges/80009/submitter">Proposal Submission</a> Deadline</strong></em>: Apri
 l 30\, 2026</p><p><em><strong><a href="https://indianauniv.ungerboeck.com
 /prod/emc00/register.aspx?aat=2f74415350355073564f6a6a762f416355574848446
 1566c746378794d6f4d4d67444739715679693634733d">Registration Costs</a></st
 rong></em><br />  Early Bird Registration: $60.00 (Before October 1\, 202
 6)<br />  Standard Registration: $80.00 (October 1 - November 15\, 2026)<
 /p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>2026 Keynote Speakers:</strong></p><p>  Alu
 ki Kotierk</p><p>  Armando Muyolema</p><p>  Diego A. Titueña Matango</p>
